1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,621
A balloon twisted in the middle.

2
00:00:02,621 --> 00:00:04,547
A wall built across a room.

3
00:00:04,547 --> 00:00:06,817
The water inside a tender coconut.

4
00:00:07,217 --> 00:00:11,582
Aarav twisted a long balloon in the
middle, and it became two balloons.

5
00:00:11,582 --> 00:00:13,792
Then he looked at a cardboard box.

6
00:00:13,792 --> 00:00:15,816
You cannot twist a box in two.

7
00:00:16,266 --> 00:00:20,673
He loves asking how things work, and
he asked: an animal cell is soft,

8
00:00:20,673 --> 00:00:22,956
but a plant cell has a stiff wall.

9
00:00:22,956 --> 00:00:25,483
So how does a plant cell split in two?

10
00:00:25,833 --> 00:00:28,571
The answer is the last
step of cell division.

11
00:00:28,571 --> 00:00:32,606
It is called cytokinesis, and plants
and animals do it in two different

12
00:00:32,606 --> 00:00:33,506
ways.

13
00:00:33,611 --> 00:00:38,075
By the end, you will explain both
ways, and say why mitosis matters so

14
00:00:38,075 --> 00:00:39,621
much to living things.

15
00:00:40,271 --> 00:00:42,064
First, what is cytokinesis?

16
00:00:42,414 --> 00:00:44,327
Mitosis divides the nucleus.

17
00:00:44,327 --> 00:00:48,964
But the cell also has cytoplasm, the
jelly around the nucleus, with all

18
00:00:48,964 --> 00:00:49,864
its parts.

19
00:00:50,295 --> 00:00:53,215
Cytokinesis is the
division of the cytoplasm.

20
00:00:53,215 --> 00:00:56,185
Cyto means cell, and
kinesis means movement.

21
00:00:56,635 --> 00:01:01,128
So karyokinesis splits the nucleus,
and cytokinesis usually follows, to

22
00:01:01,128 --> 00:01:03,130
split the rest of the cell.

23
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:05,162
Remember that word, usually.

24
00:01:05,812 --> 00:01:09,410
Let's zoom into a plant
cell, just after telophase.

25
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:13,473
Here are the cells of an onion
root tip, still dividing.

26
00:01:13,973 --> 00:01:15,526
Zoom into one cell.

27
00:01:15,526 --> 00:01:19,409
Telophase is over, so two
nuclei sit inside one cell.

28
00:01:19,909 --> 00:01:22,736
Zoom into the middle,
between the two nuclei.

29
00:01:22,736 --> 00:01:24,617
Something is being built here.

30
00:01:25,117 --> 00:01:26,553
It is the cell plate.

31
00:01:26,553 --> 00:01:30,852
It grows into the new wall, and becomes
the middle lamella, the layer that

32
00:01:30,852 --> 00:01:33,329
glues two neighbouring
cell walls together.

33
00:01:33,979 --> 00:01:37,332
Now let's watch both
ways, one after the other.

34
00:01:37,682 --> 00:01:38,582
Step one.

35
00:01:38,411 --> 00:01:39,461
An animal cell.

36
00:01:39,461 --> 00:01:43,614
A furrow, a groove, appears in the
cell membrane, around the middle.

37
00:01:44,364 --> 00:01:45,264
Step two.

38
00:01:45,051 --> 00:01:48,626
The furrow gets deeper and deeper,
until it meets in the centre.

39
00:01:49,376 --> 00:01:50,276
Step three.

40
00:01:50,249 --> 00:01:52,348
The cytoplasm is split into two.

41
00:01:52,348 --> 00:01:56,526
Organelles like mitochondria get
shared between the two daughter cells.

42
00:01:57,276 --> 00:01:58,176
Step four.

43
00:01:58,052 --> 00:01:59,015
A plant cell.

44
00:01:59,015 --> 00:02:00,896
Its wall cannot be pinched in.

45
00:02:00,896 --> 00:02:03,661
So a cell plate starts
to form in the centre.

46
00:02:04,411 --> 00:02:05,311
Step five.

47
00:02:05,241 --> 00:02:09,022
The plate grows outward, until
it meets the old side walls.

48
00:02:09,022 --> 00:02:12,221
Now there are two cells,
each with its own wall.

49
00:02:12,971 --> 00:02:16,896
Each daughter cell gets exactly
the same chromosomes as the parent.

50
00:02:16,896 --> 00:02:18,315
Why does that matter?

51
00:02:18,315 --> 00:02:19,254
Four reasons.

52
00:02:19,604 --> 00:02:20,504
One.

53
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:20,980
Growth.

54
00:02:20,695 --> 00:02:25,124
A body made of many cells, like yours,
grows by adding new cells made by

55
00:02:25,124 --> 00:02:26,024
mitosis.

56
00:02:26,286 --> 00:02:27,186
Two.

57
00:02:26,739 --> 00:02:27,639
Balance.

58
00:02:27,369 --> 00:02:31,201
As a cell grows, its nucleus
becomes too small for its cytoplasm.

59
00:02:31,201 --> 00:02:35,015
Dividing restores the nucleo cytoplasmic
ratio, the balance between the

60
00:02:35,015 --> 00:02:35,915
two.

61
00:02:35,967 --> 00:02:36,867
Three.

62
00:02:36,509 --> 00:02:37,409
Repair.

63
00:02:37,095 --> 00:02:41,368
Cells of the top layer of the skin,
the lining of the gut, and the blood

64
00:02:41,368 --> 00:02:43,412
are constantly replaced by mitosis.

65
00:02:43,912 --> 00:02:44,812
Four.

66
00:02:44,427 --> 00:02:45,327
Plants.

67
00:02:45,034 --> 00:02:49,135
Their meristems, like the apical
meristem and the lateral cambium, keep

68
00:02:49,135 --> 00:02:52,777
dividing by mitosis, so a plant
keeps growing all its life.

69
00:02:53,427 --> 00:02:56,205
Side by side, animal
and plant cytokinesis.

70
00:02:56,205 --> 00:02:57,280
How it starts.

71
00:02:57,280 --> 00:03:00,563
In an animal cell, a furrow
forms in the membrane.

72
00:03:00,563 --> 00:03:03,902
In a plant cell, a cell
plate forms in the centre.

73
00:03:04,652 --> 00:03:05,552
Direction.

74
00:03:05,375 --> 00:03:07,802
The furrow moves from the outside inward.

75
00:03:07,802 --> 00:03:10,461
The cell plate grows
from the centre outward.

76
00:03:11,211 --> 00:03:12,111
Why.

77
00:03:11,664 --> 00:03:14,820
An animal cell has a soft
membrane that can pinch in.

78
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:17,383
A plant cell has a stiff wall that cannot.

79
00:03:18,133 --> 00:03:19,033
Your turn.

80
00:03:18,944 --> 00:03:23,328
You see a new wall growing from the
middle of a dividing cell, toward its

81
00:03:23,328 --> 00:03:24,228
sides.

82
00:03:23,898 --> 00:03:25,623
Plant cell or animal cell?

83
00:03:25,623 --> 00:03:26,817
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:30,017 --> 00:03:30,917
Plant cell!

85
00:03:30,823 --> 00:03:34,920
The cell plate grows from the centre
outward, because the stiff wall cannot

86
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:35,820
be pinched.

87
00:03:36,376 --> 00:03:37,276
Remember this.

88
00:03:37,251 --> 00:03:39,140
Animals pinch from the outside in.

89
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:40,988
Plants build from the inside out.

90
00:03:41,588 --> 00:03:43,313
Now, a worked example.

91
00:03:43,313 --> 00:03:44,721
Six dividing cells.

92
00:03:44,721 --> 00:03:46,446
Furrow, or cell plate?

93
00:03:46,796 --> 00:03:48,955
A cell from the lining of your gut.

94
00:03:48,955 --> 00:03:50,793
An animal cell, so a furrow.

95
00:03:51,243 --> 00:03:53,031
A cell at the onion root tip.

96
00:03:53,031 --> 00:03:54,945
A plant cell, so a cell plate.

97
00:03:55,395 --> 00:03:57,807
A cell in the top layer of your skin.

98
00:03:57,807 --> 00:03:58,707
Furrow.

99
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:01,420
A cell in the cambium of a mango tree.

100
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:02,320
Cell plate.

101
00:04:02,746 --> 00:04:04,651
A cell in a frog embryo.

102
00:04:04,651 --> 00:04:05,551
Furrow.

103
00:04:05,796 --> 00:04:08,627
And a cell at the shoot
tip of a pea plant.

104
00:04:08,627 --> 00:04:09,527
Cell plate.

105
00:04:10,128 --> 00:04:11,592
Now, the exam trap.

106
00:04:11,942 --> 00:04:16,079
Many students think karyokinesis
is always followed by cytokinesis.

107
00:04:16,479 --> 00:04:20,544
But in some cells, the nucleus
divides and the cytoplasm does not.

108
00:04:20,544 --> 00:04:24,796
The cell gets many nuclei, called a
syncytium, like the liquid endosperm

109
00:04:24,796 --> 00:04:25,801
of a coconut.

110
00:04:26,451 --> 00:04:27,992
Where is this asked?

111
00:04:27,992 --> 00:04:32,617
As cell plate versus furrow, the
syncytium example, and why mitosis is

112
00:04:32,617 --> 00:04:33,517
important.

113
00:04:34,055 --> 00:04:35,187
Exam level now.

114
00:04:35,187 --> 00:04:37,402
Read the assertion and the reason.

115
00:04:37,402 --> 00:04:38,582
Pause and solve.

116
00:04:43,982 --> 00:04:45,253
The answer is A.

117
00:04:45,753 --> 00:04:49,994
Plant cells do form a cell plate,
which grows from the centre outward.

118
00:04:50,494 --> 00:04:54,466
They do this because the stiff wall
cannot pinch in, so the reason explains

119
00:04:54,466 --> 00:04:55,366
the assertion.

120
00:04:55,963 --> 00:04:57,503
Here is your recap.

121
00:04:57,503 --> 00:04:59,085
Screenshot this card.

122
00:05:02,285 --> 00:05:04,471
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

123
00:05:04,871 --> 00:05:05,771
One.

124
00:05:05,326 --> 00:05:08,030
How does cytokinesis
happen in an animal cell?

125
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:09,830
Two.

126
00:05:09,319 --> 00:05:11,005
What does the cell plate become?

127
00:05:11,905 --> 00:05:12,805
Three.

128
00:05:12,415 --> 00:05:14,733
Give two reasons why mitosis is important.

129
00:05:15,833 --> 00:05:19,592
Next time: why are you not an
exact copy of either parent?

130
00:05:19,592 --> 00:05:21,448
We find out in meiosis one.
